-- title:
--   Lemma 3.12 — symmetry: with a unique Nash equilibrium the errors do not depend on the player
-- statement:
--   Let $N\in\mathbb N$ and suppose that there is exactly one Nash equilibrium $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$ for the $N$-player game, with state process $\mathbf X^N$ (the solution of (2.3) driven by $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$: every solution agrees with $\mathbf X^N$ a.s. on $[0,T]$). Let $(m,\alpha)$ be a MFE with state process $X$, and let $(\alpha^i)$, $(X^i)$ be conditionally independent copies of $\alpha$ and $X$. Then for all $i,j=1,\dots,N$,
--   $$\mathbb E\Big[\int_0^T|\alpha^{N,i}_t-\alpha^i_t|^2dt\Big]=\mathbb E\Big[\int_0^T|\alpha^{N,j}_t-\alpha^j_t|^2dt\Big]\quad(3.18),\qquad\mathbb E\Big[\sup_{0\le t\le T}|X^{N,i}_t-X^i_t|^2\Big]=\mathbb E\Big[\sup_{0\le t\le T}|X^{N,j}_t-X^j_t|^2\Big]\quad(3.19).$$
--
--   It turns the bound on the sum over players in Proposition 3.16 into a bound for each player.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: the canonical spaces $\Omega^N=(\mathbb R^n)^N\times\mathcal C^{N+1}$ with product measure, embedded in one another (pp.9–10) → one probability space carrying $\xi^1,\xi^2,\dots$ (law $m_0$), $W^0$ and $W^1,W^2,\dots$, mutually independent, Brownian paths continuous (`Setup`); reason: the canonical product space is an instance, so the statement specializes to the paper's
--   - generalization: $d,k,n\in\mathbb N=\{1,2,\dots\}$ → any $n,k,d\ge 0$; reason: the source's dimensions are instances
--   - encoding: augmented filtrations $\mathbb F^N$, $\mathbb F^0$ → the raw filtrations generated by $\xi^1,\dots,\xi^N,W^0,\dots,W^N$ and by $W^0$; reason: every process progressive for the augmented filtration has a raw-progressive version ([41, Prop. 1.2.1], cited p.10); controls and states enter only up to versions
--   - encoding: continuous processes → processes with almost surely continuous paths on $[0,T]$; reason: on a general probability space continuity holds outside a null set; suprema and integrals do not see null sets
--   - encoding: players $i\in\{1,\dots,N\}$ → players `i : Fin N`, player $i$ of the paper is index $i-1$; reason: relabelling
--   - encoding: $\mathbb R^{n\times d}$ with the Frobenius norm and $A\cdot B=\mathrm{tr}(A^\top B)$ → `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin n × Fin d)`; reason: same norm and inner product
--   - encoding: a Nash equilibrium $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$ and "the state process corresponding to $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$" → `IsNashN`: admissible $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$, its state process $\mathbf X^N$ solving (2.3), compared against every admissible deviation and every state process of the deviation; and (hypothesis `hXN`) every solution of (2.3) driven by $\boldsymbol\alpha^N$ agrees with $\mathbf X^N$ a.s. on $[0,T]$; reason: p.12 defines the state process as "the solution $\mathbf X$ of (2.3)", which presupposes uniqueness; the lemma assumes no Lipschitz data, and without uniqueness (3.19) fails ($n=k=1$, $\xi\equiv0$, $\sigma=\sigma^0=0$, $b=\sqrt{|x|}$, $L=|a|^2$, $G=0$: the unique equilibrium control is $0$, and $(0,t^2/4)$ solves (2.3))
--   - encoding: $J^{N,i}$, $J_m$ real valued (footnote 1, p.12) → the costs of the equilibrium are finite, and only deviations with finite (integrable) cost are compared; reason: under the paper's assumptions every cost is finite, so the compared sets coincide
--   - encoding: $m_t=\mathcal L(X_t\mid\mathcal F^0_t)$ → $\omega\mapsto m_t(\omega)(A)$ is a.s. equal to $\mathbb P[X_t\in A\mid\mathcal F^0_t]$ for every Borel $A$ (`IsCondLaw`); $\mathbb F^0$-adaptedness of $m$ through the evaluation maps; reason: this is the definition of a version of the conditional law
--   - encoding: the copies $X^i_t=\widetilde X_t(\xi^i,W^0_{[0,t]},W^i_{[0,t]})$ of Definition 2.2 built from the representation of Lemma 2.1 → copies built from any representation (`ContRep` for $X$: copies adapted with a.s. continuous paths; `ProgRep` for $\alpha$: copies progressive); reason: Lemma 2.1 provides one, and two representations give a.s. equal copies at each $t$
--   - encoding: $\mathbb E[\sup_t|\cdot|^2]$, $\mathbb E\int_0^T|\cdot|^2dt$ → lower Lebesgue integrals in $[0,\infty]$, bound $\mathrm{ofReal}(C/N)$; reason: same value; an infinite left side violates the bound in both
--   - encoding: "exactly one Nash equilibrium" → one Nash equilibrium, and every Nash equilibrium agrees with it $dt\otimes d\mathbb P$-a.e.; reason: controls are processes up to null sets
--   - reading: "independent copies" → the conditionally independent copies of Definition 2.2; reason: the copies share $W^0$; they are only conditionally independent
-- source:
--   Jackson & Tangpi, Quantitative Convergence for Displacement Monotone Mean Field Games with Controlled Volatility, arXiv:2304.04543v2, Lemma 3.12, p.26

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CtrlVolMFG_IsSol_3_6

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped ENNReal NNReal

namespace CtrlVolMFG

/-- Lemma 3.12 (Jackson & Tangpi, arXiv:2304.04543v2, p.26): let `N ≥ 1` and suppose that there is
exactly one Nash equilibrium `αᴺ` of the `N`-player game (any other one agrees with it
`dt × dℙ`-a.e.), with state process `Xᴺ`. Let `(m, α)` be a MFE with state process `X`, and let
`(αⁱ)`, `(Xⁱ)` be conditionally independent copies of `α` and `X`. Then for all `i, j`,
(3.18) `𝔼∫₀ᵀ|α^{N,i}_t − αⁱ_t|²dt = 𝔼∫₀ᵀ|α^{N,j}_t − αʲ_t|²dt` and
(3.19) `𝔼 sup_{0≤t≤T}|X^{N,i}_t − Xⁱ_t|² = 𝔼 sup_{0≤t≤T}|X^{N,j}_t − Xʲ_t|²`.

**Formalization Note** No structural assumption is made, as on the page. `Xᴺ` is *the* state
process corresponding to `αᴺ` (p.12: "the solution `X` of (2.3)"): every solution of (2.3) driven by
`αᴺ` agrees with `Xᴺ` almost surely on `[0, T]` (`hXN`). Without it (3.19) fails: with `n = k = 1`,
`ξ ≡ 0`, `σ = σ⁰ = 0`, `b(x, a, m) = √|x|`, `L = |a|²`, `G = 0`, the unique equilibrium control is
`0`, and `(X^{N,1}, X^{N,2}) = (0, t²/4)` solves (2.3). The copies are built from any
representations `Rα`, `RX` as in Lemma 2.1. -/
theorem lemma_3_12 {n k d : ℕ} {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (S : Setup n d Ω)
    (D : Data n k d) (T : ℝ≥0) (hT : 0 < T) (N : ℕ) (hN : 0 < N)
    (αN : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k) (XN : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n)
    (hNash : IsNashN S D T N αN XN)
    (hXN : ∀ Y : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n, StateEqN S D T N αN Y →
      ∀ i, ∀ᵐ ω ∂S.P, ∀ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, Y i t ω = XN i t ω)
    (huniq : ∀ (α' : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k) (X' : Fin N → ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n),
      IsNashN S D T N α' X' → ∀ i, EqDtDP S.P T (α' i) (αN i))
    (m : ℝ≥0 → Ω → P2 n) (α : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn k) (X : ℝ≥0 → Ω → Rn n) (hMFE : IsMFE S D T m α X)
    (RX : ContRep S T X) (Rα : ProgRep S T α) :
    (∀ i j : Fin N,
      ∫⁻ ω, ∫⁻ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T,
          ‖αN i s.toNNReal ω - Rα.copy i s.toNNReal ω‖ₑ ^ 2 ∂volume ∂S.P =
        ∫⁻ ω, ∫⁻ s in Set.Icc (0 : ℝ) T,
          ‖αN j s.toNNReal ω - Rα.copy j s.toNNReal ω‖ₑ ^ 2 ∂volume ∂S.P) ∧
    (∀ i j : Fin N,
      ∫⁻ ω, ⨆ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, ‖XN i t ω - RX.copy i t ω‖ₑ ^ 2 ∂S.P =
        ∫⁻ ω, ⨆ t ∈ Set.Icc 0 T, ‖XN j t ω - RX.copy j t ω‖ₑ ^ 2 ∂S.P) := by sorry

end CtrlVolMFG
